wayland backend: capture the host pointer on a click, and give every
absolute motion its relative step

kretro runs a game in a rootful Xwayland inside this nested Weston. When
a game warps the X pointer, Xwayland locks the nested pointer and from
then on moves the X pointer by relative motion only. The stock backend
passes on absolute motion alone, so the locked pointer never moved again.

1. Every absolute motion now also carries the step from the previous one
   (WESTON_POINTER_MOTION_ABS | REL), so a locked nested pointer moves.

2. With KRETRO_WESTON_CAPTURE=1, a click in the window locks the host
   pointer (zwp_pointer_constraints_v1, one-shot) and the nested pointer
   is driven by the host's zwp_relative_pointer_v1 from then on, divided
   by the output scale. The nested compositor clamps that to its output,
   which is what keeps the pointer inside the game. The capturing click
   is not passed on. Ctrl+Alt pressed and released with nothing else in
   between releases the lock, with a cursor position hint where the
   relative motion took the pointer since the capture (added up here and
   clamped to the output, because the nested pointer stays at the warp
   point while Xwayland holds its own lock); so does the host ending the
   lock (focus loss) or the keyboard leaving. The next click captures
   again. While captured the title says how to get out, including after
   a frame is made again on the way out of fullscreen.

